README.DOC:

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

If you have downloaded the self-unpacking COP8SIM.EXE file only and are not
now using the default INSTALL.BAT installation script, you need to first
generate a $MODEL file for the National COP8 device and MetaLink product
you want to simulate.  Type the following at the DOS prompt:

       MF_GEN
               {You will then be prompted for the
                MetaLink product and National COP8 device.}
       ICESIM 

If you are installing from a diskette using the default INSTALL.BAT
installation script, MF_GEN will be invoked automatically a little bit
later in the installation process.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Description of iceMASTER COP8 Emulator / Debug Module Simulator

The ICESIM.EXE program is a simulator version of the host PC software
package with which you would interact to control a "real" MetaLink
iceMASTER emulator or Debug Module.  Rather than "talking to" an emulator or
Debug Module base unit, however, this simulator version of the host software
contains within it software modules which mimic (simulate) the behavior of
an iceMASTER hardware emulator base unit and probe card, or Debug Module.
Also bundled in is an instruction set simulator for the COP8 CPU architecture.

This version of the host software has the following properties:

    *  The software simulates the emulator as closely as possible,
       even to the point of initially asking you to which communication
       port (COM1 or COM2) the emulator is attached.  Any response will
       do;  no communication actually takes place through the serial
       communication port(s) on your computer.

    *  The emulator supported is an iceMASTER-COP8 Model 400
       emulator with 32Kb of Code memory and 4K frames of Trace
       memory.

       a)  If you would like to see how the iceMASTER Debug Module
           operates, first re-run MF_GEN to generate a $MODEL file
           for the desired Debug Module configuration (COP8 device)
           by typing the following at the DOS command prompt:

               MF_GEN

           Then, delete the current $CONFIG file by typing

               DEL $CONFIG

           Finally, reinvoke the host software by typing the
           following at the DOS command prompt:

               ICESIM -tyudm

           When simulating the iceMASTER Debug Module, be aware of
           the following:

           1)  The PROM Programmer Menu is available, but you cannot
               actually program any COP8 devices.  However, all commands
               within the PROM Programmer Menu will operate as if a device
               were actually being programmed.

    *  Since ICESIM.EXE contains within it modules which mimic the
       behavior of the emulator hardware, probe card and COP8 CPU,
       ICESIM.EXE consumes quite a bit more memory in the PC than
       the non-simulator version of the host software. This may
       constrain the size of programs (e.g., total number of
       symbols) that can be loaded into the simulator.

    *  External events and interrupts (e.g., timer interrupts) are
       not simulated/supported. Access to a port is treated as an
       access to the port register, not the port pins. Timers act
       as simple registers (i.e., they do not count automatically).
       Basically, the "chip" simulator built into this system
       simulates the COP8 CPU instruction set, but does not
       simulate any peripheral functions of the COP8 chip.

    *  When this "emulator simulator" is placed into emulation (as
       if executing code in the COP8 processor), the execution time
       reported will be the same as a 10MHz COP8 executing the same
       code. However, since the execution of each instruction is
       actually being simulated in software, the elapsed wall clock
       time will be significantly greater than the execution time
       displayed in the Status Window.

    *  The sampling rate of the Performance Analyzer is much lower
       than that in an actual iceMASTER emulator hardware base
       unit. Again, this is because the execution of each COP8
       instruction is being simulated in software.

    *  When beginning execution from the RESET condition, you
       should select the Run|Reset|Emulator command.

    *  The Probe Bit values captured in the Trace Buffer are from a
       simple 7-bit counter. All other captured values (e.g.,
       address and data) accurately reflect the execution flow
       within the simulated COP8 CPU.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

The unpacked files from the Distribution Diskette in this release of
the Emulator Simulator Host Software for the COP8 family of processors are:

    README.DOC     The file you are now reading.

      === Host Software Files ===

    ICESIM.EXE     Emulator Simulator Host Software executable program.

    $CLR1$         File containing default video display color and 
                   highlighting values suitable for color CGA/EGA/VGA monitors.

    $CLR2$         File containing default video display color and 
                   highlighting values suitable for color EGA/VGA monitors.

    $CLRL$         File containing default video display "color" and 
                   highlighting values suitable for LCD monitors.

    $CLRM$         File containing default video display "color" and 
                   highlighting values suitable for monochrome monitors.

    MF_GEN.BAT     Utility program used to generate a $MODEL file to allow
    MF_GENN.EXE    simulation of a particular emulator probe card or Debug
                   Module.  The emulator simulator host software (ICESIM.EXE)
                   reads this $MODEL file during its initialization sequence.
                   During installation of the Distribution Diskette, MF_GEN
                   will be invoked automatically to create the $MODEL file for
                   the emulator probe card or Debug Module that you want to
                   simulate.  MF_GEN may be re-invoked manually, if ever
                   necessary, to regenerate the $MODEL file or to generate a
                   different $MODEL file (for a different probe card or a
                   different configuration).
 
    MODELS.DAT     Data file read by the MF_GEN utility program.  This file
                   contains the information necessary to generate a $MODEL
                   file for each available probe card and configuration.
                   Note that this file is not used during actual operation
                   of the host software (ICESIM.EXE).  You can safely delete
                   this file after you have generated the $MODEL file(s)
                   for the probe card(s) / configuration(s) you plan to use.
 
    MFG.EXE        This program is called only from MF_GEN.  MFG should
                   never be invoked directly at the DOS command line prompt.

    $CR_C880       Emulator Control RAM initialization files. The host
    $CR_C888       software may automatically download one of these files
    $CL0           (depending on which probe card or Debug Module you
    $CL1           are using) into the simulated emulator base unit during
    $P6            initialization.

      === Demonstration Programs ===

      --- C8A1.COF:  Assembly Language Demo Program ---
      --- (National COP8 Assembler/Linker)          ---

    C8A1.COF      Linker-generated absolute, loadable COFF file
                  (with debug information)
    C8A1MAKE.BAT  Batch file to create loadable C8A1.COF file from scratch
    C8A1MAKE.LNK  Linker control file for creating C8A1.COF
    C8A1INC.INC   COP8 demo program, Include File (COP8 assembly source)
    C8A1MAIN.ASM  COP8 demo program, Module #1    (COP8 assembly source)
    C8A1SPIN.ASM  COP8 demo program, Module #2    (COP8 assembly source)

      --- BC82MAIN.COD: Byte Craft C Demo Program ---

    BC82INNR.C    Function 'innerloop()' (C source)
    BC82MAIN.C    Main program (function 'main()';  C source)
    BC82MAIN.COD  Loadable, absolute object module format,
                  (with debug information)
    BC82MAIN.HEX  Loadable HEX file for BC82MAIN
    BC82MAIN.SYM  Compiler-generated symbolic information file for BC82MAIN
    BC82MAKE.BAT  Batch file to generate BC82MAIN.COD from scratch
    BC82PRAG.H    C #include file
    BC82WAST.C    Function 'wastetime()' (C source)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Beginning with Version 3.0 Revision 12, the host software utilizes not only
code overlaying, but also data overlaying.  This capability is provided by the
'.RTLink/Plus' linker (.RTLink/Plus is a trademark of Pocket Soft, Inc.).
The advantage of overlaying data as well as code is that the host software
consumes less memory during execution.  This provides more memory for you
to load larger programs with full symbolic information.

During execution of the host software, the .RTLink/Plus overlay manager swaps
overlay sections out to temporary files on disk.  If you have the environment
variable 'TMP' defined, .RTLink/Plus uses the directory designated by this
variable.  If the 'TMP' environment variable is not defined, .RTLink/Plus
places the temporary files in the root directory.  In either case, the
temporary files all have the extension '.TMP'.

If, during execution of the host software, there is not enough available
disk space for .RTLink/Plus to create one of the temporary files,
you will see the messages:

     eca0001: .RTLink CACHE - Save File Handling
     DOS Function code 0040, Error code FFFF

In some cases, the first line above will not be displayed and you will see
only the 'DOS Function code' message.  In either case, the message(s) may
display anywhere on the screen.  When this happens, the host software has
been terminated and you may even need to reboot your computer.  Delete any
residual '.TMP' files and ensure that you have enough available disk space.
We have found that approximately 400K-800K of disk space should be free
before invoking the host software.

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Software Setup:  Environment Variables 'TMP' and 'TEMP':

The 'TMP' and 'TEMP' environment variables are used not only by the host
software program, but also by many other programs.  Each designates the
directory where an executing program's temporary files should be created.
These temporary files typically are for the program's internal use and are
usually deleted when the program terminates.  To determine where these
temporary files should be created, some programs use the environment
variable 'TMP', others use the environment variable 'TEMP'.  It is safest
and easiest to set both environment variables, 'TMP' and 'TEMP', and have
them designate ("point to") the same directory on your computer.  You can
do this by adding the following statements to your AUTOEXEC.BAT file:

      SET TEMP=C:\TMP
      SET  TMP=C:\TMP

It is important that the designated directory ('C:\TMP' in this case)
actually exists on your computer.  You can create this directory by
typing:

      MKDIR C:\TMP

If the designated directory does not exist on your computer, the host
software will probably terminate with the following message displayed
somewhere on the screen:

     Fatal Error evm0013:  Error in VM save file handling
                           (Probably out of disk space).

If you choose to have separate directories for 'TMP' and 'TEMP', you
would add something like the following to your AUTOEXEC.BAT file:

      SET TEMP=C:\ABC
      SET  TMP=C:\XYZ\ZYX

and type the following to create the designated directories:

      MKDIR C:\ABC
      MKDIR C:\XYZ
      MKDIR C:\XYZ\ZYX

Note that the actual directory path(s) and name(s) can be whatever you
choose.  The only critical items are the names of the environment
variables ('TMP' and 'TEMP') set to designate those directory path(s)
and name(s).

In a networked environment, make sure that you have "create" and "write"
permissions on the drive and directory that you designate for both 'TMP'
and 'TEMP'.

=== End of:  README.DOC ===
